Riverton Aviation Discussion

Issued at 2152 UTC Saturday, August  9, 2025



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z SUNDAY/...

Issued at 351 PM MDT Sat Aug 9 2025

A dry weather system will slide through the region today into 

tomorrow, which is bringing increased cloud cover to the area. Most 

of this cloud cover is around FL150 and above. Isolated virga 

showers are possible this evening, mainly confined to central 

mountains and southern Wyoming. Wind will generally remain around 10-

15 knots from the northwest to northeast this evening before 

weakening overnight. Isolated showers and storms will develop in 

Natrona and Johnson Counties Sunday afternoon. There is around a 20% 

of impacts at KCPR, so no inclusion of -TSRA or -SHRA at KCPR at 

this time.

Please see the Aviation Weather Center and/or CWSU ZDV and ZLC for 

the latest information on icing and turbulence forecasts.



Cheyenne Aviation Discussion

Issued at 2346 UTC Saturday, August  9, 2025



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z MONDAY/...

Issued at 541 PM MDT Sat Aug 9 2025

Stubborn stationary front will linger along the mountains between

Rawlins and Laramie late this evening before eventually pushing

south into Colorado. Although the drier airmass will prevent

convection across much of the area, a few thunderstorms are possible 

further east near KSNY. Thunderstorms and showers will increase in 

coverage Sunday for all areas east of KRWL. 

HAZARDS/WEATHER TRENDS: VFR conditions expected tonight for all 

terminals with the exception of KSNY. KSNY could see another round 

of thunderstorms this evening before skies gradually clear. Recent 

models are hinting at some fog/low CIGS near KSNY and KIBM by early 

Sunday morning, so started hinting at IFR conditions in the TAF. 

Otherwise, all terminals (outside of KRWL) will see showers and 

thunderstorms developing in the afternoon hours and possibly linger 

into the evening. IFR VIS due to heavy rain and gusty winds are 

possible with the activity.





