[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.1623","issue":"2025-08-10T00:58:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS61","cccc":"KRNK","awipsid":"AFDRNK"}]
=================================================



707

FXUS61 KRNK 100059

AFDRNK



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Blacksburg VA

Issued by the National Weather Service Raleigh NC

858 PM EDT Sat Aug 9 2025



.SYNOPSIS... Surface high pressure will gradually weaken through 

allowing for warming temperatures and fair weather. Warmer 

weather and daily chances for afternoon and evening showers and 

thunderstorms return for the upcoming work week.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH SUNDAY/...

As of 858 PM EDT Saturday...



A few showers developed just north of Greenbrier County WV, but should be 

dissipating soon. Forecast on track and should see fog in the river

valleys especially in the Greenbrier and New River Valleys late tonight.



Previous Discussion:



Key Messages: 



1) Fair weather expected through Sunday. 



High pressure will gradually weaken over the area through

tomorrow. Easterly flow has strengthened slightly, allowing 

clouds to develop in an adequately moist environment as winds 

interact with the terrain. After sunset clouds will mostly 

dissipate. Some stratus is possible over the southern Blue Ridge

towards Sunday morning, but most locations will see clear skies

or cirrus overnight. Some patchy fog may develop in river and 

mountain valleys with light ESE winds continuing, with no rain 

expected.



Overnight, temperatures in the 50s and 60s are expected across

the region. Sunday will be 3 to 5 degrees warmer than today, 

with highs in the upper 70s to low 80s. Rain is still not 

expected through tomorrow evening, although precipitation will 

edge towards NW NC.



Forecast confidence is moderate to high.



&&



.SHORT TERM /SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY NIGHT/...

As of 145 PM EDT Saturday...



Key Messages:



1) Increased moisture and temperatures



2) Daily afternoon storms



As a wedge completes its erosion by Monday, a subtropical gyre

will the become settle over the deep southern CONUS. This will

position us with plenty of southwesterly flow off the Gulf 

area. Temperatures and dewpoints will rise quickly over the 

first couple days of the week. In addition, this will increase 

the instability of the environment, and without the suppression 

of the wedge, diurnal showers and storms will begin to form each

afternoon and evening. Most of the activity will remain south 

of the I-64 corridor for this portion of the forecast, as the 

driving factor behind this convection will be the 

destabilization of the boundary layer due to daytime insolation.



Highs will reach into the upper 70s west of the Blue Ridge, and

well into the 80s east.



&&



.LONG TERM /WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...

As of 130 PM EDT Saturday...



Key Messages:



1) Temperatures remain warm



2) Increased areal coverage of storms and showers 



A subtropical ridge will remain over the southeastern CONUS, 

continuing to supply tropical moisture and warm air advection to

the Mid-Atlantic. A longwave trough will move through the OH 

Valley to our north, providing some dynamical support for 

convection throughout the forecast area. As this trough moves 

off the coast, it will reintensify, increasing the mid-level 

vorticity, and providing further support into the late week. 

Over the weekend, a shortwave will move near the area, 

continuing the trend of wide areal coverage of thunderstorms in 

the forecast area. Temperatures will remain above normal during 

this portion of the forecast.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

As of 858 PM EDT Saturday...



VFR through much of the period at all terminals. Exception will be

some MVFR to IFR fog late tonight at all terminals except ROA

with VLIFR at LWB. Fog lifts between 12-14z with VFR through the

rest of the day Sunday.



.EXTENDED AVIATION OUTLOOK...

We will be heading into a more typical summer pattern with

late night/early morning fog and daily chances for shower

and storms in the afternoon/evening. Aside from storms

and fog, expect VFR.





