778

FXUS61 KCAR 100102

AFDCAR



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Caribou ME

902 PM EDT Sat Aug 9 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

High pressure centered east of NewFoundland will ridge back 

across the Gulf of Maine tonight into early next week. A cold 

front will approach from the northwest on Wednesday and slide 

through the area late week.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH SUNDAY NIGHT/...

900PM Update: No major changes to the forecast. Updated the 

beach hazard statement for Sunday.



Previous Discussion...

High pressure centered east of NewFoundland will ridge back 

across the Gulf of Maine tonight through Sunday night. Aloft,

upper level ridging builds across the region tonight through

Sunday night. An isolated shower/thunderstorm is possible 

across mostly higher terrain areas this evening, which will

rapidly end with the loss of diurnal heating. Haze is also

possible across northern areas early tonight. Otherwise, 

generally expect mostly clear/partly cloudy skies across the 

region tonight. However, patchy fog is also possible along the 

Downeast coast late tonight. With diurnal heating, isolated

showers/thunderstorms are again possible across mostly the 

north and mountains Sunday afternoon. Otherwise, expect mostly

clear/partly cloudy skies. Low temperatures tonight will range 

from around 60 to the lower 60s north, with mid to upper 50s

Downeast. High temperatures Sunday will range from the mid to 

upper 80s north, to the lower to mid 80s interior Downeast with

upper 70s to around 80 along the Downeast coast. Low 

temperatures Sunday night will range from the lower to mid 60s 

north, with around 60 to the lower 60s Downeast.



&&



.SHORT TERM /MONDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...

A 500mb upper level low remains cutoff southeast of Nova Scotia

with a 593dam high situated over Maine. Monday will be Hazy, 

Hot and Humid. Expect a hot one as 850mb temps warming to +19C 

to +20C across the area resulting in widespread surface air 

temperatures in the 90s. Expecting most locations 91-95F from 

the St. John Valley to Eastern Aroostook down I-95 to Bangor 

Region and Interior Downeast including Calais. Upper 80s to near

90F for the North Woods and Moosehead Region. A sea breeze 

along the Downeast coast will turn winds SSW 5-10mph and keep 

highs in the 80s along Route 1 and 70s along the shoreline. 

Mostly sunny skies will be hazy with a mix of increased moisture

in the air and smoke aloft that is continuing to swirl around 

the Northeast. Dew points increase into the 60s to around 70F so

expecting a muggy day. Given that increased moisture heat index

values will be in the mid to upper 90s from the eastern St. 

John Valley southward in the Crown to the entire I-95 corridor 

and southern areas of the Central Highlands then into interior 

Downeast Maine. Heat headlines will likely be needed for much of

the CWA North and West of Route 9 including the Bangor area 

along I-95 to Houlton and points northward. Expecting moderate 

to major heat related impacts in these areas. These are 

conditions that affects anyone without effective cooling and/or 

adequate hydration. Impacts likely in some healthy systems, 

heat- sensitive industries and infrastructure. 



Monday night will be a muggy night with temperatures falling

back into the mid 60s across much of the area with low 60s at

the shoreline thanks to the colder Gulf of Maine waters. Patchy

fog will develop across the typical areas inland like over

lakes, ponds and in river valleys. Along the Downeast coast

expecting with the light south winds and colder water a fog bank

to develop and push inland overnight. Fog may become dense

overnight resulting in reduced visibility along the Downeast

coast.



Heading into Tuesday expecting a slight weakening of the 500mb 

high to around 590dam overhead. 850 temps remain around +20C so

expecting yet another Hazy, Hot and Humid day. Dew points in the

60s to around 70F in spots, perhaps 1-2 degrees cooler given

slightly drier air than Mon but very unlikely to be that 

noticeable. Widespread 90s for high temperatures once again

except 70s to 80s Downeast thanks to the seabreeze. Head index

values once again climb widespread 94-98F likely requiring Heat

Headlines once again for a large portion of the CWA. 



In terms of fire weather concerns. The ongoing abnormally dry

conditions will continue to deteriorate. We are seeing very dry

fuels across the area especially Central Highlands to the

Downeast Coast. Winds each day generally 5-15mph with afternoon

minimum RHs in the 40-50 percent range. Isolated areas of 35-40

percent across the Moosehead Region into the North Woods.



&&



.LONG TERM /TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...

Overall...dry weather is the overall trend through next weekend

with only precipitation chances being scattered showers and 

thunderstorms Wednesday into Thursday. Precipitation will be 

highly localized to who gets a thunderstorm. Given this trend 

expecting rapidly worsening drought conditions in much of the 

CWA as a flash drought continues to develop. 



Tuesday night expecting another muggy night with temperatures

taking till well after midnight to fall into the low to mid 60s.

The 500mb ridge weakens on Wednesday but still hazy, hot and

humid with highs topping out in the upper 80s to low 90s.

Slightly lower dew points in the low to mid 60s expected given

the approaching shortwave allows for better mixing. Expecting

clouds to increase from the NW as a cold front pushes into the

St. Lawrence River valley into the afternoon hours. Timing

differences still remain between the globals like GFS, ECMWF,

ECMWF AIFS and Canadian. Decent chance on Wednesday for showers

and thunderstorms to develop in response to the approaching

front and shortwave. Modeled soundings showing 1000-1500j/kg of

SBCAPE to develop with even GFS suggesting up to 2000j/kg. Will

need to monitor trends as current Corfidi Upshear values of

5-10 suggest training slow moving storms as there is timing

differences on the arrival of increase shear. Given the deep

warm cloud layer and inverted V signature from strong mixing

suggests potential for gusty winds and isolated heavy 

downpours. These storms would mainly be confined to the 

Moosehead, Baxter SP and Danforth areas northward with greatest 

risk in the North Woods to Caribou region. 



Models differ on timing but overall show the front slowly

drifting south through the state on the day Thursday. This will

result in dew points falling behind the front as it pushes

south. Will continue a slight chance to chance of showers and

perhaps a storm or two on Thursday but overall CAPE values look

weak. Less muggy for most locations especially behind the front

so highs in the upper 70s to low 80s north with low to mid 80s

for the southern 1/2 of the CWA. 



Friday into Saturday another 500mb ridge builds into the area

and keeps us dry. Highs a few degrees either side of 80F with

lows generally in the 50s warmer south and cooler north.



&&



.AVIATION /01Z SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...

NEAR TERM: Variable conditions with any fog, mostly Downeast,

later tonight and again Sunday night. An isolated

shower/thunderstorm possible across the north and mountains 

Sunday afternoon. Otherwise, VFR. South/southwest winds 5 to 10

knots tonight, 10 to 15 knots Sunday, then 5 to 10 knots again 

Sunday night.



SHORT TERM: Mon...AM IFR/LIFR vsby possible due to FG. Otherwise

expect VFR. HZ may reduce vsby between 6-8SM. SW winds 5-10kt 

north terms, S 5-10kt at south terms. 



Tue...AM IFR/LIFR vsby possible due to FG. Otherwise VFR. HZ may

reduce vsby between 6-8SM. SW winds 5-10kt north, S-SW winds

5-10kt south. 



Wed...AM IFR/LIFR vsby possible due to FG. VFR cigs. S-SW winds

5-15kt. -SHRA/VCTS in the afternoon for PQI, CAR and FVE.

Generally VFR with possible VCTS at HUL. No precip expected BGR

and BHB. 



Thu...VFR. VCSH possible. North terms to south terms will see 

winds shifting 5-15kt.



&&



.MARINE...

NEAR TERM: Winds/seas below small craft advisory levels tonight

through Sunday night. Patchy fog tonight into Sunday night. 



SHORT TERM: Winds/seas will be below SCA criteria through the

week. Fog may reduce vsby at times from Mon-Wed especially

overnight and morning hours. Seas generally 1-3ft this week.

S-SW winds through this week will shift late week.



&&



.CLIMATE...

Record high temperatures are possible in some locations both 

Monday and Tuesday.



Records Highs for Monday Aug 11:



Caribou: 92 in 2020 

Houlton: 91 in 2020

Millinocket: 96 in 1944

Bangor: 99 in 1944



Record Highs for Tuesday Aug 12:



Caribou: 93 in 1944

Houlton: 92 in 2020

Millinocket: 94 in 1944

Bangor: 100 in 1944



&&



.CAR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

ME...Beach Hazards Statement from 8 AM EDT Sunday through Sunday 

     evening for MEZ029-030.

MARINE...None.



&&



$$





Near Term...LaFlash/Norcross

Short Term...Sinko

Long Term...Sinko

Aviation...LaFlash/Norcross/Sinko

Marine...LaFlash/Norcross/Sinko

Climate...



