AFDMAF



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Midland/Odessa TX

607 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



...New AVIATION...



.KEY MESSAGES...

Updated at 602 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



- Cooler temperatures and increased storm chances (20-50%)

  continue through Tuesday. The main threats for the strongest

  storms will be gusty winds, frequent lightning, small hail, and

  heavy rainfall leading to localized flash flooding for a few

  locations. 



- Temperatures begin to climb back above normal as thunderstorms

  become largely confined west of the Pecos River valley by the 

  latter half of the week.



&&



.SHORT TERM...



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Wednesday through Sunday)

Issued at 256 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



Wednesday, an upper-level trough departs and ridging begins to take 

shape over the Desert Southwest. Meanwhile, another upper-level 

ridge shall rest to our east, centered near the southeastern CONUS.

This western ridge will continue to develop and translate eastward 

through the remainder of the week, resulting in generally warm and 

dry conditions. Temperatures Wednesday are forecast to top out in 

the upper 80s over the higher elevations, 90s for most everyone, and 

100s along the Rio Grande. Thursday afternoon, the upper ridge 

becomes centered near the New Mexico/Colorado border. Thursday's 

highs kick up a few degrees over Wednesday's, heating into the 100s 

along the Rio Grande and within much of the Trans Pecos, while areas 

elsewhere reach into the 90s. Heading into the weekend, the ridge 

looks to continue an eastward path, expanding all the while. 

Temperatures this upcoming weekend mainly reach into the 90s each 

afternoon, with 100s along the Rio Grande. In terms of rainfall, low 

to medium (15-40%) rain chances are forecast over the region 

Wednesday afternoon, with the best odds over the higher terrain. 

Through the rest of the period, rain chances shift to the western 

half of the region, primarily west of the Pecos River. 



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 602 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



VFR conditions are prevailing this evening with southeast winds

across the region. Earlier thunderstorms that impacted MAF have

moved off to the south of the area. Additional thunderstorms are 

beginning to develop across Southeast New Mexico with possible 

impacts to HOB and CNM through the remainder of this evening. A 

separate round of thunderstorms may reach CNM and HOB overnight, 

but confidence is overall low, though highest for CNM on 

potential impacts. Winds gradually shift to a more easterly 

direction through the overnight hours across the region. 

Additional thunderstorms are possible during the afternoon on 

Tuesday, but this will be towards the very end of the TAF period 

and remains highly uncertain.



&&



