2025-08-11T23-17-40-008Z

[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8559","issue":"2025-08-11T23:17:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KLBF","awipsid":"AFDLBF"}]
=================================================



251

FXUS63 KLBF 112317

AFDLBF



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service North Platte NE

617 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- After rain chances end tonight, a few days of mostly dry

  conditions can be expected. 



- Heat will build into the area Wednesday through Friday. Some

  increase in humidity could lead to the possibility of heat

  headlines. 



- Some increase for precipitation chances by the weekend as

  monsoonal moisture moves northward into the region.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THROUGH WEDNESDAY NIGHT/...

Issued at 334 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



A remnant MCV continues to slowly move eastward across 

southwest/southern Nebraska this afternoon. Ascent is best 

immediately ahead of this feature across south central into portions 

of central Nebraska. Carried some slight pops for lingering evening 

showers/thunder across portions of southwest/central Nebraska early 

this evening before ending. 



A weak cold front settles southward through the area Tuesday. 

Surface high pressure will nose down through the Missouri River 

Valley, with northeast winds gradually becoming southeast as the 

high settles down the Missouri toward Omaha Tuesday night. 

Temperatures won't be cooler behind the front, but dew points will 

lower somewhat with mostly 50s expected area wide through the day 

Tuesday.  



West-northwest flow aloft increases somewhat Wednesday. An embedded 

shortwave within the flow will approach the Dakotas by late 

afternoon. Surface low pressure will deepen across eastern Wyoming 

ahead of this feature. Southerly low-level flow will increase in turn 

across the local area. Ascent increases by afternoon across the 

western Dakotas, and appears to be a favorable set up for a 

southeastward moving MCS Wednesday night. We'll have to monitor how 

far south this gets. Have included some low chance pops at this 

point across north central Nebraska.



&&



.LONG TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...

Issued at 334 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



Ridging aloft attempts to build northward across the southern 

Rockies Wednesday, then translates eastward across the central and 

southern plains Thursday and Friday. This will bring rising 

temperatures to the area, with highs in the 90s. Humidity will be on 

the increase Thursday and especially by Friday. Could see dew points 

back to 70F or higher, especially east of Highway 83. Heat index 

values could approach or exceed 100F and heat headlines may be 

needed. 

  

As the upper high becomes anchored across Missouri area Saturday and 

Sunday, monsoonal moisture will flow northward on the western 

periphery of the high. This will bring a return chance of 

showers/thunder to the area as some of this moisture reaches our 

area. Highs will cool a bit during this time, but humidity will be 

high. Highs will generally be in the upper 80s and lower 90s through 

the weekend.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z TUESDAY/...

Issued at 617 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



Isolated showers continue to diminish early this evening.

Expect VFR conditions and light winds AOB 10KTs tonight through

Tuesday afternoon at the KLBF and KVTN terminals. 



&&



.LBF WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

None.



&&



$$



SHORT TERM...Taylor

LONG TERM...Taylor

AVIATION...Roberg



