414

FXUS65 KTFX 112326

AFDTFX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Great Falls MT

526 PM MDT Mon Aug 11 2025



Aviation Section Updated.



.KEY MESSAGES...



- An isolated shower or thunderstorm is possible tonight over the

  Rocky Mountain Front and across the mountain areas between Great

  Falls and Lewistown.



- It turns very warm, windy, along with lower humidity for 

  Tuesday and Wednesday.



- Seasonable August temperatures, along with mostly dry conditions

  are expected from Thursday through the upcoming weekend.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

/Issued 225 PM MDT Mon Aug 11 2025/ 



 - Meteorological Overview:

A weak upper level disturbance will move southward through North

Central MT this evening. This disturbance could produce a few

showers/thunderstorms along the Rocky Mountain Front and across

the mountain areas between Great Falls and Lewistown.



For Tuesday and Wednesday...warmer air will move northward into

the CWA, allowing for a return of very warm afternoon

temperatures. Afternoon highs will climb into the 90s for lower

elevation locations. Additionally, do expect lower humidity and

winder conditions during the afternoon hours. There will be a

chance for thunderstorms across Southwest MT late Tue afternoon

into Tue evening as an upper level disturbance moves through that

portion of the state. 



For Thursday through Monday...generally a warm westerly flow aloft

will reside over the entire northern Rockies during this period.

This will result in seasonable August temperatures most days. In

terms of precipitation, weak upper level disturbances will move

through the CWA from time to time, with small chances for a

passing shower/thunderstorm as the disturbance moves through.

Overall, no widespread significant precipitation events are

expected over our CWA at this time during this period.



 - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:

Afternoon temperatures will be on the warm side for Tue and Wed,

along with lower humidity and stronger winds. This will increase

fire concerns, especially for grassy areas. Other than the fire

weather watch for Southwest MT for Wednesday, no other fire

statements are planned for areas further north because of the

recent precipitation.



For thunderstorm potential for Tue afternoon/evening...some of 

the storms could produce some strong wind gusts, with the Dillon 

to Bozeman area the most favored area right now per the HREF 

model. Brusda



&&



.AVIATION...

12/00Z TAF Period 



There is a small chance for an isolated storm this evening over

the Rocky Mountain Front and across the mountains of Central MT,

from Great Falls to Lewistown. Otherwise, expect dry conditions,

with passing areas of mid/high clouds. On Tuesday, expect surface

winds to increase during the afternoon hours, with a small chance

for a passing storm after 21z Tue, mainly affecting areas

along/south of I-90 from Butte to Bozeman. Gusty winds/brief heavy

rainfall/lightning will be the main impact from any storms 

tonight or on Tue. Brusda



Refer to weather.gov/zlc for more detailed regional aviation 

weather and hazard information.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Expect warmer and drier conditions to develop over the districts

over the next few days. Also, winds will increase, especially by

Wednesday. This will result in elevated fire weather conditions

for grassy areas.



A fire weather watch was issued for portions of Southwest MT for

these conditions on Wednesday. Brusda



