[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8935","issue":"2025-08-11T23:30:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS65","cccc":"KFGZ","awipsid":"AFDFGZ"}]
=================================================



628

FXUS65 KFGZ 112330

AFDFGZ



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Flagstaff AZ

430 PM MST Mon Aug 11 2025



.SYNOPSIS...Hot and mainly dry conditions persists through the

start of this week. A few isolated showers and thunderstorms will

be possible each afternoon, primarily from the Mogollon Rim and

south. Better chances for showers and thunderstorms look to 

potentially return by the middle to end of this week, along with 

cooler temperatures.



&&



.DISCUSSION...High pressure currently sits over California.

This is positioned to bring a dry northwesterly flow to our area, 

and we expect storms to stay fairly constrained to the southern half 

of Arizona today and tomorrow. Dry surface dewpoints and low PW will 

keep storm coverage isolated each day, though a few stronger storms 

with hail and gusty outflow winds are possible. Any activity will

be primarily along and south of the Mogollon Rim. The ridge 

begins to shift east over Arizona while declining in magnitude 

tomorrow. The result is a few more days of hot temperatures, 

particularly towards far northern AZ where it is driest.



The ridge continues to push eastward, opening a  window to allow 

moisture to return. This is complicated by the fact that another 

trough will manifest over California and potentially disrupt the 

flow of subtropical moisture. The trough is acting as a major 

point of uncertainty in ensemble guidance, and model spread is 

high on PW and especially surface Td. Even the lower end scenarios

are still bringing increased coverage of showers and 

thunderstorms across northern AZ on Thursday and Friday, with 

moisture penetrating as far north as the Kaibab. By Saturday the 

25th-75th spread in surface dewpoints reaches 10-15 degrees, and 

it becomes much more difficult to make definitive statements on 

thunderstorm activity. The general consensus is that activity 

will tick down starting Saturday then more so Sunday, but this 

can certainly change as the synoptic features become clearer in 

future forecast periods.



&&



.AVIATION...Tuesday 12/00Z through Wednesday 13/00Z...VFR conditions. 

ISO -SHRA/-TSRA through 02Z, mainly along and south of a KFLG-KSOW

line. Gusty outflow winds possible in and around TS. W-NW winds 10-15

kts gusting to 25 kts, becoming variable less than 10 kts overnight.



OUTLOOK...Wednesday 13/00Z through Friday 15/00Z...VFR conditions. 

ISO-SCT -SHRA/-TSRA on Wednesday mainly along and south of a 

KGCN-KSOW line. Gusty outflow winds possible in and around TS. 

W-NW winds 10-15 kts each day, becoming less than 10 kts overnight.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...Tuesday and Wednesday...Hot, breezy, and mostly dry 

conditions will continue through Tuesday. Isolated to scattered 

thunderstorms (10-30% chance) are possible along and south of I-40. 

Daytime humidity will drop to 5-15% north of I-40 and 10-20% south. 

Moisture begins to increase on Wednesday with scattered thunderstorm 

coverage (30-60%) expected from the Grand Canyon and Mogollon Rim 

southward. Daytime winds will be west to northwest at 5-15 mph with 

light winds expected overnight.



Thursday through Saturday...The brief increase in moisture will lead 

to increasing humidity and better rain and thunderstorm chances 

through Friday. Temperatures will decrease through this period with 

west to southwest winds around 10-20 mph each day.  Moisture and 

thunderstorm chances will begin to decrease on Saturday.



&&



.FGZ WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Thursday for AZZ006.



&&



$$



PUBLIC...Lewandowski

AVIATION...Meola

FIRE WEATHER...MCT



For Northern Arizona weather information visit

weather.gov/flagstaff



