[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8998","issue":"2025-08-11T23:33:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS65","cccc":"KPIH","awipsid":"AFDPIH"}]
=================================================



691

FXUS65 KPIH 112333

AFDPIH



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Pocatello ID

533 PM MDT Mon Aug 11 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Warming trend through mid-week, with above normal highs on 

  Wednesday.

 

- Isolated showers and thunderstorms in the mountains Wednesday

  and Thursday. Much stronger winds by Wednesday.



- Critical fire weather conditions for much of the region

  Wednesday and Thursday due to low humidity and gusty winds.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THROUGH TUESDAY NIGHT/...

Issued at 133 PM MDT Mon Aug 11 2025



A high pressure system over the west coast and trough departing 

eastward over the central U.S. will maintain northwest flow aloft 

through late tonight/early Tuesday. Upper-level flow will turn zonal 

beginning Tuesday as a shortwave shifts through the PacNW and high 

pressure amplifies over the southwest. The warming trend will 

continue over the next couple of days, with near-normal temperatures 

today and highs several degrees above climatological norms Tuesday, 

bringing valley highs into the low to mid-90s. Winds will remain 

light today, becoming breezy Tuesday afternoon ahead of the front, 

with gusts 20 to 25 mph.



&&



.LONG TERM /WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...

Issued at 133 PM MDT Mon Aug 11 2025



Wednesday still looks like the peak of this warm spell, 

with highs hitting the mid 90s over a good chunk of central and 

eastern Idaho. We also see the ridge breaking down and the flow 

going westerly. It looks breezy with gusts hitting 20-35 mph during 

the afternoon and evening. At the moment, we won't hit any trigger 

points for wind-related headlines except in the fire weather world. 

For the trifecta, we do see a small surge of moisture that is enough 

for a few thunderstorms across the higher elevations. With how dry 

it is, don't expect much if any rain out of most storms that 

far...although a stronger storm could eek out enough rainfall to 

measure in the gauge. Beyond that, we end up setting up in southwest 

flow as the typical summertime monsoon pattern TRIES to set up. What 

is typical about it will be the ridge centered near the Four 

Corners. What isn't typical is the lack of moisture in this setup, 

which has been the case so far this summer across the Southwest U.S. 

and into our area. There will be just enough for some isolated storm 

in the mountains, with the best overall chance across the South 

Hills/Albion Mountains into the southeast highlands. We will likely 

need to watch day to day for any potential stronger push for 

scattered storms in that area, or overall better chance for isolated 

coverage elsewhere. Temperatures do drop back a few degrees with 

some anticipated increase in clouds and storm potential. Our Blend 

of Models is still show 80s and low 90s for highs through the 

weekend.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z WEDNESDAY/...

Issued at 533 PM MDT Mon Aug 11 2025



Forecast confidence is high in dry, mostly clear/sunny, VFR 

conditions over the next 36 hours with no major impacts to aviation. 

Winds at KSUN will likely shift SE to SW during Tuesday afternoon.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 133 PM MDT Mon Aug 11 2025



A high pressure system over the west coast and trough departing 

eastward over the central U.S. will maintain northwest flow aloft 

through late tonight/early Tuesday. Upper-level flow will turn zonal 

beginning Tuesday as a shortwave shifts through the PacNW and high 

pressure amplifies over the southwest. The warming trend will 

continue over the next couple of days, with near-normal temperatures 

today and highs several degrees above climatological norms Tuesday. 

Relative humidity will largely remain above 15 percent today, with 

fairly widespread sub-15 percent values beginning Tuesday. Winds 

will become breezy Tuesday afternoon ahead of the incoming trough 

but will largely remain below critical threshold, with gusts 

generally in the 20 to 25 mph range. The warming trend will continue 

through mid-week as temperatures climb to well above normal by 

Wednesday under high pressure, with most valley locations likely to 

observe low to mid-90s. On Wednesday, the ridge will break down and 

winds will become stronger Wednesday and Thursday, with gusts in the 

20 to 35 mph range. These two days will be critical fire weather 

days for much of the region due to the combination of low relative 

humidity and gusty winds. In additional to elevated winds, 

temperatures will begin to cool and the east and southeast mountains 

and highlands will have slight chances for isolated showers and 

thunder through late week, especially across Fire Weather Zones 411, 

425, and 427.



&&



.PIH WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

None.



&&



$$



SHORT TERM...Cropp

LONG TERM...Keyes

AVIATION...01

FIRE WEATHER...Cropp



