2025-08-11T23-33-44-112Z

[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.9006","issue":"2025-08-11T23:33:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS66","cccc":"KOTX","awipsid":"AFDOTX"}]
=================================================



699

FXUS66 KOTX 112333

AFDOTX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Spokane WA

433 PM PDT Mon Aug 11 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Moderate and Major HeatRisk through Tuesday.



- Elevated to critical fire weather conditions Tuesday and  

  Wednesday with warm, dry, and breezy conditions.



- Cooling trend through end of week with showers for the weekend.



&&



.SYNOPSIS...

A warming trend will provide more typical summer like conditions

over the next few days, with the warmup peaking Tuesday as high

temperatures warm into the high 90s to low 100s. Breezy winds 

will usher in a gradual cool down Wednesday through Friday. 

Showers expected to return for the weekend.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Monday night through Tuesday: A weak upper level ridge will

continue to bring very warm temperatures across the Inland

Northwest through Tuesday. Low temperatures will be warmest

across central Washington and the L-C Valley with lows in the 

upper 60s to mid 70s and upper 50s to low 60s for far eastern 

Washington and north Idaho. The upper level ridge begins to

retrograde on Tuesday, but will have little impact on the

temperatures across the Inland Northwest with highs in the mid

90s to low 100s. Widespread Moderate and Major HeatRisk will

increase the risk for heat-related illnesses, especially for

those vulnerable to heat and those without effective cooling 

and hydration. 



The retrograding ridge will allow for a shortwave to slide

southeastward and bring a return of onshore flow into western 

Washington on Tuesday. An increase in the cross-Cascade pressure

gradient will bring breezy winds through the Cascade gaps and 

the western Columbia Basin in the late afternoon and the 

evening. These conditions will result in a period of elevated 

and localized critical fire weather conditions. Upslope

component of the winds into the east slopes of the Cascades look

to hold off the strongest winds until after 4PM with relative 

humidity values rising above critical values during the peak of 

the winds in the evening. However, there is a 50% chance for 

pockets of critical fire weather conditions around Wenatchee, 

Entiat, and the Waterville Plateau between 4PM-8PM. This is not 

high enough confidence for fire weather highlights at this time,

but people should still be cautious to not cause any sparks. 



Wednesday: Models are in good agreement for a shortwave to 

round the base of an upper level trough over British Columbia on

Wednesday with a dry cold front sweeping through the Inland 

Northwest. This will be the most favorable day for critical fire

weather conditions with westerly winds 15 to 25 mph, wind gusts

25 to 35 mph, and relative humidity values in the upper teens 

to low 20s in the lee of the Cascades through the Columbia Basin

and eastern Washington. Temperatures will cool by 5-10 degrees 

between Tuesday and Wednesday with widespread highs in the upper

80s to mid 90s. The exception will be for the L-C valley with 

temperatures remaining in the upper 90s.



Thursday through Sunday: There is solid agreement for the

Pacific Northwest to remain in an active weather pattern through

the end of the week into the weekend with broad troughing over

the region through Thursday, and then a deeper upper level low

developing in the Gulf of Alaska Friday digging southeastward 

along the BC coast through the weekend. Precipitation chances 

begin to increase across western Washington Friday morning, 

spreading eastward Friday afternoon. Between 5 AM Friday and 5 

AM Sunday, the NBM is giving far northeastern Washington and 

north Idaho a 50% chance of 0.50 inches of precipitation. This 

decreases further south with a 15% chance for 0.50 inches for 

Spokane. There is a 30% chance for 0.10 inches for Spokane 

during this same period. Nonetheless, this will be a period of 

widespread cooler and moist weather which will dampen fire 

weather conditions. /vmt



&&



.AVIATION...

00Z TAFS: VFR conditions will prevail with light winds in most 

areas. Westerly winds at KEAT winds ramp up around 23z to near 

15kt. 



FORECAST CONFIDENCE AND/OR ALTERNATE SCENARIOS:

High confidence for VFR conditions. 



-----------------------



Confidence descriptors:

Low - Less than a 30 percent chance

Moderate - 30 to 70 percent chance

High - Greater than a 70 percent chance



For additional probabilistic information for NWS Spokane

airports, please refer to the Aviation Dashboard on our webpage:

https:/weather.gov/otx/avndashboard



&&



