=================================================



759

FXUS64 KLIX 112337 AAA

AFDLIX



Area Forecast Discussion...UPDATED

National Weather Service New Orleans LA

637 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



...NEW AVIATION...



.SHORT TERM...

(This evening through Wednesday) 

Issued at 312 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



Convection began to slowly develop across coastal sections of 

SELA and near the Southshore but as the storms propagated to the 

south earlier in the day blow off from these storms helped to 

spread a decent bit of cirrus over the area again and much like 

yesterday has had some impact on afternoon highs. This also has 

kept storms from becoming a little more scattered across the area.

If convection doesn't fire in the next hour then there will likely

not be much if any storms overnight for inland areas. If storms

can develop or move in from the east they will likely persist

through a good chunk of the evening. 



The next 2 days should see quite a bit more convection. With a weak 

sfc low over the coastal waters just southeast of our waters storms 

will likely consolidate more over the waters overnight with a decent 

bit of convection. As a weak easterly wave in the northeastern Gulf 

move into the area tomorrow convection should become more 

widespread. Moisture will increase with PWs well abv 2" possibly 

around 2.25" while mid lvl hghts fall 1-2dm. This wave will also 

bring a slight increase in the mid lvl flow and looking aloft we 

will move under the RRQ of the jet leading to enhanced divergence 

aloft. With that convection should have a much easier time tomorrow 

especially along and south of a line from just south of BTR to BOG 

where LL convergence will be the strongest. This day may have the 

best chance of leading to localized flash flooding as storms will be 

fairly efficient and with an inverted trough laying up WSW to ENE 

across the southern half/southeastern half of the area tomorrow 

there is a LL focus. Storm motion will be rather slow and with the 

potential of backbuilding storms leading to training if storms line 

up over the wrong areas there could be some problems. 



Wednesday is a little more messy as we begin to lose some of the LL 

focus but mid lvl forcing is still in place as the wave is still 

moving through the area. H5 winds will still be a steady 20 kts out 

of the south. Upper lvl divergence is still in place just weaker 

than on Tuesday. PWs as expected remain around 2.2". With that 

numerous to widespread convection is possible again but it would not 

be surprising to see more like sct convection especially if there is 

a lot of cloud cover impacting daytime heating. /CAB/



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Wednesday through Sunday night)

Issued at 312 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



The back and forth continues between periods of numerous to 

widespread showers followed by days of isolated to widely 

scattered convection and much warmer temps. Medium range models 

and their respective ensemble means are in agreement with 

thunderstorm activity slacking off by the end of the work and 

through the weekend. NBM looks pretty good overall...PoPs aren't 

too high and temps are in the ball park...maybe a touch too cool 

for morning lows and perhaps a degree or two on highs but highs 

will be highly dictated by amount of convection and cloud cover. 

With that no changes were made to the extended portion from the 

latest NBM at this time. 



Thursday will be the next transition day as high pressure begins to 

build back into the area from the east becoming centered over the 

area late Friday and through the weekend. On Thursday we should 

still see scattered to numerous storms as out easterly wave 

continues to push west. Friday through the weekend the building 

ridge will lead to rising hghts and thus increasing suppression 

across the region but this is building in from the east and not the 

west. Because of that this likely won't be enough to completely shut 

off convection. Overall moisture remains similar around 2" or 

greater, dewpoints remain in the 70s, and mid lvl temps look to 

range from -5 to slightly cooler than -7C. Given this PoPs will 

generally remain in the 30-40% range Friday through the weekend. 

This will have some impact's on temps. LL temps will slightly 

increase with h925 temps of 26-27C this weekend and h85 temps around 

20-21C. This should equate to mid 90s maybe even a few sites topping 

out around 97 but the problem becomes cloud cover and convection as 

both of these could keep temps down a notch or two. With that we 

will continue to hold onto the lower 90s to 95/96 for highs in the 

forecast. Morning lows are the biggest question mark. There is no 

real dry air pushing into the region so LL/BL moisture will remain 

high and with afternoon highs Friday through the weekend a touch 

higher morning lows may struggle to get out of the upper 70s for 

much of the area. Not raising morning lows yet for this weekend but 

may need to bump these up a few degrees over the next couple of 

days. /CAB/



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 632 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



VFR conditions expected overnight and into Tuesday morning.

Convection coverage will increase on Tuesday by afternoon,

especially for the eastern most terminals. GPT may experience some

early convection that may move in from the sound. Expect lower

VIS/CIGs during showers and storms. Winds could also be a bit

higher in and around convection Tuesday afternoon, otherwise,

mostly 10kts or less southerly winds expected. (Frye) 



&&



.MARINE...

Issued at 312 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



Broad area of high pressure at the sfc centered over the western 

Atlantic will continue to dominate the Gulf. This will lead to 

persistent light winds out of the south and southwest. As typical 

during the Summer expect slightly higher winds overnight east of 

the MS river. Biggest issue for the marine community will be 

convection especially during the overnight and early morning 

hours. Gusty winds and waterspouts will be possible with these 

storms. /CAB/



&&



.PRELIMINARY POINT TEMPS/POPS...

MCB  74  90  73  89 /  20  70  20  70 

BTR  76  91  75  90 /  30  70  20  70 

ASD  74  88  73  90 /  50  80  30  70 

MSY  79  91  78  91 /  40  80  30  70 

GPT  76  87  75  89 /  60  70  40  70 

PQL  74  87  74  90 /  60  70  50  70 



&&



.LIX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

LA...None.

GM...None.

MS...None.

GM...None.

&&



$$



