[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.9166","issue":"2025-08-11T23:42:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KDLH","awipsid":"AFDDLH"}]
=================================================



861

FXUS63 KDLH 112342

AFDDLH



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Duluth MN

642 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Showers and thunderstorms will move through the region this

  evening and tonight. A few stronger storms will be possible in

  western areas.



- Cooler and less humid conditions expected for Tuesday and

  Wednesday.



- Chances for showers and thunderstorms return Thursday and into

  the weekend with some stronger storms possible at times as

  well.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 349 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



High pressure was in place across the western Great Lakes this

afternoon with cold front to both the east and west. The cold

front to the west will be responsible for showers and

thunderstorms later this evening and tonight as it moves

through. Smoke was spreading across the Northland as well with

reduced surface visibilities and hazy skies reported across much

of the region. The smoke will linger into the this evening

before being cleared out overnight by the cold front.



Storms are forecast to develop later this afternoon across the

Red River Valley ahead of the cold front and push east into the

Northland by mid to late evening. Weak moisture pooling ahead of

the front and a shortwave trough aloft will aid in this

development as well. CAPE up to 1000 J/kg and bulk shear around

25 knots will help storms to organize a bit, but an overall

linear storm mode is expected. Anemic mid-level lapse rates and

skinny CAPE profiles are not favorable for large hail

development. A few stronger wind gusts will be possible across

Koochiching, Itasca, northwestern St. Louis, Cass and

northwestern Crow Wing counties with DCAPE around 500 J/kg and 

winds of 50-60 knots aloft. This area is in a marginal risk from

the SPC which seems appropriate given the limited threat. As 

the line of showers and storms pushes east overnight, it is 

expected to diminish with northwest Wisconsin likely not seeing 

much. As the upper trough swings through Tuesday morning, 

additional showers and perhaps a few rumbles of thunder will be 

possible across northeastern Minnesota.



Cooler temperatures will filter in behind the cold front Tuesday

with highs Tuesday and Wednesday in the 70s. Another slug of

smoke will likely move through the region during this time as

well with northwest flow aloft. With surface high pressure in

place, this smoke may mix to the surface as well.



A weak shortwave will move through the Upper Midwest on Thursday

in zonal flow aloft leading to chances for showers and

thunderstorms. The setup looks similar to tonight and the SPC

has almost the same area outlooked on Day 4. Gusty winds looks

to be the primary threat with these storms along with some small

hail. As temperatures warm into the 80s by Friday with dewpoints

rising into the 60s and lower 70s, another impulse will crest

the upper ridge and bring more chances for showers and storms.

Given the better moisture and heat expected Friday, better

chances for strong to severe storms are likely. The weekend then

looks to remain active in terms of rain chances with 

temperatures cooling back into the 70s.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z WEDNESDAY/...

Issued at 637 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



A line of thunderstorms will produce scattered thunderstorms and

numerous rain showers in north-central Minnesota beginning

around 02Z this evening and lasting into the early overnight

hours. A few strong thunderstorms cannot be ruled out as far 

east as INL to BRD through 05Z with small hail and erratic wind 

gusts to 40 knots possible. As the line moves into northeast

Minnesota 04-06Z, a the line will quickly decrease in strength

and intesity of the precipitation. By the time it enters

northwest Wisconsin only scattered rain showers are forecast

from 06-12Z Tuesday. Behind the responsible cold front for the

line of storms, a quick wind shift will occur to scour out much

of the current round of Canadian Wildfire smoke. So expect

improving visibilities from 06-12Z and lasting until about 

19-21Z Tuesday. Tuesday later afternoon will then feature the

next round of smoke moving southeastward again from Canadian

Wildfires that lasts into Wednesday.



&&



.MARINE /FOR NEAR SHORE WATERS OF WESTERN LAKE SUPERIOR/...

Issued at 349 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



Southwesterly winds of 5 to 15 knots will continue for tonight

and Tuesday. Some stronger gusts to 20 to 25 knots along the

North Shore Tuesday from mainly Grand Marais to Grand Portage 

may lead to conditions hazardous to small craft. Winds then turn

more westerly to northwesterly Tuesday evening before becoming

variable at 10 knots or less for Wednesday. 



A cold front will move across western Lake Superior late tonight

into Tuesday morning. Not much is expected in turns of a wind

shift. Storms should be weakening by the time they reach Lake

Superior, but some gusty winds to around 20 to 30 knots cannot

be ruled out.



For the open water discussion, refer to the NWS Marquette Area

Forecast Discussion at weather.gov/mqt.



&&



.DLH WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

MN...None.

WI...None.

MARINE...None.



&&



$$



DISCUSSION...BJH

AVIATION...NLy

MARINE...BJH



