AFDABQ



Area Forecast Discussion...UPDATED

National Weather Service Albuquerque NM

549 PM MDT Mon Aug 11 2025



...New AVIATION...



.KEY MESSAGES...

Issued at 122 PM MDT Mon Aug 11 2025

 

- Marginal risk of severe thunderstorms east of the central

  mountain chain this afternoon and evening.

 

- Moderate to high risk of flash flooding below recent burn scars

  today and Tuesday, then moderate on Wednesday. 



- Scattered virga showers and isolated dry thunderstorms will be

  capable of producing strong to erratic wind gusts of 40 to 50

  mph over portions of west central and central NM this 

  afternoon.



- After decreasing coverage of showers and thunderstorms Wednesday

  and Thursday, storm coverage will increase over central and

  western areas again at the end of the week.



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(This evening through Tuesday night)

Issued at 122 PM MDT Mon Aug 11 2025



As an upper level trough and accompanying shortwave progress 

eastward through NM (as shown on satellite imagery and RAP height 

analysis), scattered to numerous thunderstorms are expected to 

develop along and east of the central mountain chain. A few storms 

may become strong to severe this afternoon and evening, given SBCAPE 

values are forecast to rise up to 2000 J/kg with 30-35kts of bulk 

shear. Large hail and damaging winds are the primary threats with 

any stronger storms. CAMs differ a bit on initiation location and 

timing, but are in agreement on a cluster of thunderstorms forming 

across far eastern NM by late afternoon to mid evening, which may 

increase a wind threat if this cluster can become an organized line. 

Alongside of strong to severe thunderstorms, heavy rainfall is 

likely in any storm, and slower storm motions (10-20mph) across 

eastern NM may lead to localized flash flooding. Elsewhere, near the 

Ruidoso area, cumulus clouds have begun to develop as of 1230pm and 

isolated showers have begun to develop south near Cloudcroft. CAMs 

continue to disagree whether storms will develop over or east of the 

burn scars, and that has limited confidence in whether a burn scar 

flash flood threat will materialize this afternoon, but with the 

threat there, the current Flash Flood Watch will continue. In 

western NM, PWAT values drop significantly (below 0.5"), and outside 

of breezy winds and the occasional virga shower, mostly clear skies 

are expected.



With the crop of storms expected in eastern NM later today, they are 

very likely to push one or several outflow boundaries up against the 

central mountain chain tonight. This works to create gusty gap winds 

through the ABQ and Santa Fe metro areas, where gusts up to 40mph 

may occur after 9pm. As the gap winds and boundaries work to push 

moisture further west, this sets the stage for increased 

precipitation chances along and west of the central mountain chain 

for Tuesday. Storm motions also look to be slower and directed more 

to the south. A stray strong to severe storm may occur, though shear 

is noticeably weaker, inhibiting updrafts from sustaining 

themselves. Rather, the slow moving nature of storms leads to 

another potential round of burn scar flash flooding near the Ruidoso 

area, and another Flash Flood Watch has been issued for this. 

Confidence is higher for storms forming near the burn scars given 

moisture will have wrapped around the Sacramento Mountains. 

Elsewhere, the HPCC scar and off-scar locations have a low chance 

for localized flooding. 



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Wednesday through Sunday)

Issued at 122 PM MDT Mon Aug 11 2025



Throughout Wednesday and the rest of the work week, the upper level 

high that had shifted over to CA this past weekend now works its way 

back through NM. It is likely to be weaker than when it gave us very 

hot temperatures, and will also not completely eliminate our 

moisture. Thus, afternoon rounds of isolated to scattered 

thunderstorms are likely across the high terrain. Chances for 

precipitation are slightly higher across the Gila Region given 

embedded disturbances circling the high interacting with slightly 

higher moisture content. As we progress in the weekend, models and 

ensembles have been keen on developing a broad trough to our west 

and a high to our east, allowing a more traditional monsoon plume to 

cover NM. The highest precipitation chances are currently painted 

for Friday, given that the approaching trough is forecast to have a 

shortwave progressing through its axis, giving additional support 

for synoptic scale lift. This should also increase storm motions, 

limiting flash flooding to only the heaviest of storms. There may be 

a risk of training and/or multiple rounds of storms, but confidence 

is not high to definitively say. Afternoon thunderstorms are likely 

to continue into and through the weekend.



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 543 PM MDT Mon Aug 11 2025



Scattered showers and thunderstorms will continue to push into 

eastern New Mexico with some strong to severe cells producing

damaging downburst winds, large hail, and heavy downpours through

the early evening. Storms should mostly die off by 12/0400UTC.

Strong and gusty east canyon winds will develop in vulnerable

central areas of New Mexico, including at KSAF and KABQ. Gusts

will exceed 35kt at KABQ, so an Airport Weather Warning has been

issued through 12/1000UTC. These east winds will send moisture

farther west, so storms will favor more central areas of the state

going into Tuesday afternoon. Scattered to numerous slow-moving 

heavy downpours are expected Tuesday between the Continental 

Divide and the central mountain chain.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 122 PM MDT Mon Aug 11 2025



Elevated fire weather are likely through this afternoon and early 

evening across western NM due to a combination of low teen to single 

digit humidity and breezy winds. After today, elevated to critical 

fire weather conditions are not expected for the next several days. 

Moisture seeps through the central mountain chain tonight, resulting 

in a gusty gap wind up to 35kts. This moisture continues to move 

westward throughout the week, raising humidity values across western 

NM. Winds remain light, and the chances for wetting rainfall in 

western NM increases as the week progresses, especially Friday.



&&



