=================================================



461

FXUS63 KBIS 112358

AFDBIS



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Bismarck ND

658 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Showers and thunderstorms will be found through this evening.

  An isolated severe thunderstorm is possible across the eastern

  half of North Dakota.



- Strong to severe thunderstorms could return to parts of the

  region Wednesday and again Thursday.



- Expect highs in the mid 70s to mid 80s through this week, 

  although it could be as warm as the lower 90s in the southwest

  on Wednesday and south central on Thursday.



&&



.UPDATE...

Issued at 648 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



Widely scattered showers and thunderstorms continue across much

of the northwest and central. Per SPC analysis, the environment

is generally characterized by around 500 to 1000 J/kg MLCAPE 

and 30 knots of effective shear. Thus far, storms have stayed 

fairly tame, but brief heavy downpours and small hail will

remain possible under the heaviest cores. The chance for a

severe storm or two around the James River Valley is low but not

zero. This activity should start to diminish as we lose daytime

heating, gradually tapering off through the evening.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 345 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



Upper level trough with an associated cold front will push

across the state this evening. SPC has maintained a Marginal

(level 1 of 5) Risk for severe weather through this evening.

Surface and MUCAPE is modest this afternoon and evening, with

shear also modest. The shear vector is angled to this boundary,

thus multi cells will be the main storm mode this evening.

Hodographs also showing not much rotation is possible to these

storms, and the lack of mid level lapse rates could also bring

smaller hail size. DCAPE is also lacking this evening, limiting

the wind threat. Yet there is still just enough shear and

instability to produce an isolated stronger storm with hail to

the size of a quarter and wind to 60 mph being the main threats.

Some breezy winds may accompany the passage of this front

through this evening. Smoke should push eastward and out of the

area behind this front as well. Shower and thunderstorms

diminish tonight, with the steady northwest wind limiting the

chance for fog tonight. Lows in the 50s are generally forecast.

Tuesday, weak ridging in northwest flow will keep conditions

mainly clear and dry. Look for slightly cooler temperatures in

the 70s for Tuesday. The northwest flow could return smoke to

the state, although current HRRR smoke forecast keeps the near

surface smoke to the eastern portions of the state, thus left

out of the forecast for the time being. Mainly clear conditions

then continue for Tuesday night, with light winds becoming

southerly.  



Wednesday and Thursday could still see the return for chances of

at least isolated severe weather although uncertainty remains.

Wednesday days sees a warm front build back into the area, with

high temperatures back in the 80s for most sites. SPC has

shifted the Marginal Risk southward more into South Dakota for

Wednesday. Instability still looks to be modest to higher with

modest amount of shear as well. The southeasterly flow could

also advect higher dewpoints into the area. Thus the ingredients

for severe weather will return. The lift will be the uncertainty

as some models have shifted the surface low and trough southward

today. The southern half of the state could still see strong to

severe thunderstorms, although some uncertainty on location

remains. There could also be a few isolated thunderstorms in 

the west, although dewpoints may be less in these areas limiting

the severe weather threat. SPC has maintained a 15% chance 

outlook for Day 4 (Thursday). Better instability and shear look

to be found on this day, with a better life mechanism found 

with a passing cold front. Timing of this cold front will play a

big role on where and when thunderstorms can develop. Right now

the eastern half of the state has the higher chances for strong

to severe thunderstorms. Warm temperatures will remain ahead of

this front, with highs in the 80s to perhaps lower 90s. Behind 

this front will only see highs in the 70s, mainly in the 

northwest. Cooler low temperatures may also be found Thursday 

night into Friday morning with lower 50s northwest to lower 60s 

southeast. 



Friday through the weekend, zonal flow looks to remain across

the region during this time period. Highs will generally be in

the mid 70s to mid 80s which for some areas is normal, while

others is below normal. Near daily slight chances for showers

and thunderstorms will also be found through the weekend,

although may be dependent on any weak waves in the zonal flow.

Later in the weekend could see a warm front lift across the

area, which may increase thunderstorm chances somewhat. CSU-MLP

currently has low chances for severe weather this weekend. A

general southerly flow could also bring some increased dewpoints

this weekend, keeping humidity values elevated and low

temperatures in the 50s to perhaps 60s later in the weekend.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z WEDNESDAY/...

Issued at 648 PM CDT Mon Aug 11 2025



Scattered showers and thunderstorms will continue through the

evening hours, gradually tapering off. If a heavier storm moves

overhead at any given site, brief MVFR visibilities and

gusty/erratic winds will be possible. As we start to lose

daytime heating, this activity should diminish, giving way to

VFR conditions through the rest of the period. With the

scattered nature of the convection, we decided to utilize PROB30

groups over the next few hours. Winds will remain breezy out of

the west or northwest. 



&&



.BIS WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

None.



&&



$$



UPDATE...ZH

DISCUSSION...Anglin

AVIATION...ZH



